Intuition
For every element, we have two choices: either include it in the subset or exclude it.
We can use recursion to explore both choices and generate all possible subsets.
Approach
Use backtracking to generate subsets.
At each index, first include the current element and make a recursive call.
Then backtrack by removing the last added element.
Make another recursive call by excluding the current element.
When the index reaches the length of the array, add a copy of the current subset to the result list.
Complexity
Time complexity: O(n * 2^n) — There are 2^n possible subsets, and copying each subset takes O(n) time.
Space complexity: O(n) — For the recursion stack and temporary subset, excluding the space used to store the result.
Code
class Solution {
    List<List<Integer>> ans = new ArrayList<>();

    public List<List<Integer>> subsets(int[] nums) {
        List<Integer> temp = new ArrayList<>();
        findsubsets(nums, temp, 0);
        return ans;
    }

    public void findsubsets(int nums[], List<Integer> temp, int i) {
        if (i == nums.length) {
            ans.add(new ArrayList<>(temp));
            return;
        }
        temp.add(nums[i]);
        findsubsets(nums, temp, i + 1);
        temp.remove(temp.size() - 1);
        findsubsets(nums, temp, i + 1);
    }
}
